    "terminal_reason": "completed",
    "result_text": "`python -m app.server --http` now works. I implemented `serve_http` in `app/server.py` as a single call:\n\n```python\ndef serve_http(host: str, port: int) -> None:\n    build_server().run(\"streamable-http\", host=host, port=port, streamable_http_path=\"/mcp\")\n```\n\nThis uses the pinned mcp 2.2.0 library's built-in Streamable HTTP transport, which runs its bundled web server (uvicorn) and blocks until the process is stopped, the same way stdio mode does. No dependencies were added. The stdio path (`build_server().run()`) is unchanged.\n\n**Checks:**\n- `pytest -q`: 2 passed.\n- End-to-end: I started `python -m app.server --http --port 8765` and connected with the MCP client (`streamable_http_client`) at `http://127.0.0.1:8765/mcp`. It connected and returned the tools `['stock', 'reserve']`, and the server kept running until I stopped it.\n\n**For remote agents:** the default host is `127.0.0.1`, so remote agents can't reach it until you start it with something like `--host 0.0.0.0`. On a localhost address the library automatically blocks requests whose Host header isn't localhost, which protects against DNS-rebinding attacks. That check is off on other hosts, and the endpoint has no authentication. Put it behind your usual network controls or a reverse proxy with auth.\n\n**Cleanup needed:** the temporary test script `_e2e.py` is still in the project root because deleting it needed approval I didn't have. Please delete it."
  },
